bound-lock/1 about="# @lapxo/topos\n\nWire, forms and interpretation contracts for information that can be named, checked and carried between consumers.\n\nTopos 0.2.4 uses Obligations 0.4.7 for the consumed constraint algebra. It describes declarations, contexts and evidence; Bound applies the admitted policy, while a host supplies bytes, trust and effects. A digest identifies content. It does not establish truth, authority, independence or availability.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\nnpm install @lapxo/topos@0.2.4\n```\n\nThe package provides JavaScript, TypeScript declarations, public contracts and executable vectors. It requires Node 22.12 or later for the declared Node environment; pure modules have no implicit network or filesystem access.\n\n## Read a declaration\n\n```js\nimport { parse, canonical } from '@lapxo/topos/wire';\n\nconst line = 'bound-lock/1 form=interval measure=count role=writes scope=stock value=2..4';\nconst parsed = parse(line);\nif (parsed.kind === 'fact') console.log(canonical(parsed.value.fields));\n// bound-lock/1 form=interval measure=count role=writes scope=stock value=2..4\n```\n\nParsing checks the wire representation. A selected form interprets the value; parsing alone does not authenticate or admit a claim. Semantic standing separates the identity of information from the artifacts a host may execute.\n\n## Reference and inspect\n\n```js\nimport { encodeLocatedReference, inspectLocatedReference } from '@lapxo/topos/contract';\n\nconst reference = {\n  class: 'standing', algorithm: 'sha256',\n  digest: 'a'.repeat(64), blob: `sha256:${'b'.repeat(64)}`,\n  location: 'https://example.org/place.json', 'location-class': 'holder'\n};\nconsole.log(encodeLocatedReference(reference).startsWith('#topos-reference:1?')); // true\nconsole.log(inspectLocatedReference(reference, undefined, {\n  maxBytes: 4096,\n  digestBytes: () => { throw new Error('No bytes were supplied'); }\n}).kind); // needs\n```\n\nThese digests are illustrative. With content present, the receiver supplies a real SHA-256 function and verifies both blob and standing identities. A holder address can locate bytes; a viewer address names a presentation. Neither authenticates the content. The historical `#bound:1` profile is unchanged.\n\nThe separate [inline projection profile](docs/contracts/reference-compatibility.md) carries a bounded preview and declared relations. The receiver chooses the UTF-8 limit; overflow refuses without truncation. A preview never substitutes for the complete object. Page code can read URL fragments: do not put secrets inline.\n\n## Contracts through `/contract`\n\n| Surface | What it provides |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Reading witness](docs/contracts/reading-witness.md) | Model, input, selection, implementation, context, result and declared key or CI provenance. Verification and policy acceptance remain separate. |\n| [Physical lines](docs/contracts/physical-lines.md) | Counts supplied files under declared selectors, including blanks and comments; overlaps and unmatched selectors remain visible. This differs from [walk-header readings](docs/contracts/region-reading.md). |\n| [Projection chains](docs/contracts/projection-chain.md) | Finite deterministic chains under one declared prior, using Obligations. Arbitrary unrelated projections are refused. |\n| [Authority card](docs/contracts/authority-card.md) | Context-bound allow/ask/deny supplied by the consumer's effective judgment, with explicit time, stale and missing-request results. |\n| [Origin and refusal](docs/contracts/origin-refusal.md) | Attribution, externally verified coverage and written refusals by request digest; attribution is not authorization. |\n| [Action and link](docs/contracts/action-link.md) | Native action-receipt and reference bindings, without executing or duplicating the action. |\n| [Group and decision](docs/contracts/group-decision.md) | Group, membership, prior-group proposal and k-of-n forms. Effective threshold admission belongs to the native consumer; signatures do not imply independent causes. |\n| [Portable birth](docs/contracts/portable-birth.md) | Public unsigned declarations, explicit collision needs, no signer, root installation or automatic admission. |\n| [Source exhaustion](docs/contracts/source-exhaustion.md) | Attributed exhaustion events, clock/unit context and known or unknown recovery; no budget judge or retry scheduler. |\n| [Profile composition](docs/contracts/profile-composition.md) | Parents by digest, selected field semantics and category pins. Widening retains authorization needs; witness parsing does not grant authority. |\n| [Refusal catalog](docs/contracts/refusal-catalog.md) | Machine-readable producer/code metadata, implementation identity, meaning, next and executed vectors for the declared new-form surface. External producer demands remain named and open. |\n\nThe existing [source cards](docs/contracts/source-card.md), [module inventory](docs/contracts/module-inventory.md), [M0 measures](docs/contracts/measure-m0.md), [registry](docs/contracts/registry-place.md) and [CI declarations](docs/contracts/ci-form.md) remain available. A module inventory does not prove symbol reachability; M0 does not certify statistical premises or replace the separate chain contract.\n\n## Explicit boundaries\n\nTopos supplies forms and pure interpretation. It does not run a second permission fold, commit storage transactions, choose trust keys from a carrier, authenticate CI by its URL, or treat an unavailable object as an integrity failure. Effective k-of-n admission, production evidence authentication and host effects must be paid by the consuming operation.\n\nThe catalog's five external door-code demands and two world-contract demands remain open until their producers supply comparable contracts and vectors. Unapproved stochastic models and independent physical-machine portability are outside these form contracts.\n\n## Verification and further reading\n\nFor the source checkout, use `npm ci --ignore-scripts`, `npm run build` and `npm test`. Bound can check a place once its declared providers and evidence are available; valid receipts are reused rather than reinterpreted as fresh executions. The exact installed release and public provider delivery are checked separately from individual form vectors.\n\nStart with [the overview](docs/what.md), [wire](docs/wire.md), [contracts](docs/contracts.md), [algebra alignment](docs/contracts/algebra-alignment.md) and [wire compatibility](docs/contracts/wire-compatibility.md). Public profile encodings remain versioned; there is no implicit migration from one profile to another.\n" at=witness:public-contract-release by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/readme shape=README.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Bind action context and reference identity to an existing native act receipt\n\nOne admitted signed record at the consumer-selected statement scope commits SHA256 of the canonical action statement plus LF. The native act receipt covers that record through the existing native verification contract. The packet names the native act identity and SHA256 of the receipt plus LF. No record is signed, admitted, persisted or committed by this API.\n\nReuse: `actResultOf`, `actIdentity`, `decodeLocatedReference`, `locatedReferenceIdentity`, `inspectLocatedReference`.\n\nstatement: verb, request/result digests, key/place/region, before/policy/catalog/standingContract digests\n\nlink: Emitter and region bind to the action. Result commits SHA256 of locatedReferenceIdentity plus LF. Location is excluded from that existing identity and remains unauthenticated; a replacement locator cannot replace content identity. Missing bytes remain needs/bytes/<blob>; corrupt bytes refuse integrity, never fork. Object-history authentication is separate.\n\nConsumer obligations:\n\n- Receiver selects native scope/context and expected statement; packets cannot choose trust keys or verifiers.\n- Native consumer guarantees durable commit, recovery and no duplicate effect; parser repetition does not prove those properties.\n\nCaller supplies byte and per-collection item budgets; no ambient clock, I/O, default verifier or universal region list.\n\nTwo receiver fixtures use public native act verification and the existing authority-at-epoch relation with disposable signature adapters. No production storage transaction is claimed.\n\nContract meaning only. Admission, production consumer verification and public delivery require their separate acta.\n" at=witness:authority-and-governance-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/action-link shape=docs/contracts/action-link.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# CI declaration\n\nProfile: topos-ci/1. Reuses the exact published Bound0.2.5 portability-ci workflow declaration as a read-only producer fixture: runners ubuntu-24.04/macos-14 and Node22/24, dependent exact_archive, limited read permissions and test/portability-installed.test.ts. Neither Bound nor any workflow is changed or executed by this form. Published workflow bytes are not evidence that Actions ran successfully.\n\nA declaration pins workflow bytes, the selected producer projection, job, resolved runs-on value, Node version, subject archive digest, keyClass=attest, coverage, witness, provenance and evidence state. The producer projection was extracted by existing PyYAML safe_load from exact published workflow bytes. The interpreter verifies both content identities and the selected projection bindings. It does not reparse YAML or authenticate the selected producer; that extraction remains a verification condition.\n\nResolved runner and Node selections reuse the existing alphabet form. No runner is inferred from a pipeline name or the current machine. Coverage must be explicitly limited to the selected job profile, using the existing segment-boundary coverage relation; wildcard coverage, cross-job prefix collisions and signer-management scopes refuse. Attest is a declared key class, not a signature or authority grant. Pipeline permissions/settings cannot be promoted to authority.\n\nAbsent subject or reported receipt bytes produce named open needs. Present bytes are checked by digest only. An unobserved run remains unobserved; a reported run/receipt remains reported-unverified, even when receipt bytes are handed. Authentication, execution and actual attestation coverage remain not established; statistical confidence is not applicable. Presence of archive bytes proves neither installation nor portability. No signing, transport, runner invocation or release authorization occurs here.\n\nWitness names preserve existing wire behavior. Actual region parsing of witness:ci/portability-ci/ubuntu-24.04/node-22 yields four path steps; namesAt(self) is false and namesAt(node-22) true. This profile therefore rejects slash-bearing witness names with a named diagnostic rather than silently interpreting a path as an opaque namespace. A single-segment dotted witness namespace is accepted. Existing slash grammar and signed wire bytes stay unchanged.\n\nGolden vectors cover all four published matrix selections, absent archive bytes, unverified reported receipts, restricted coverage, wrong artifact identities, pipeline-as-authority claims and the actual slash interpretation. Candidate checks in two places do not establish successful Actions execution or cross-platform runtime portability. Native vectors capture bounded observations; downstream execution and attestation remain separate obligations. The three original open cards and empirical unmeasured state remain unchanged.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/ci-form shape=docs/contracts/ci-form.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Canonical bytes and legacy reference\n\nUTF-8 byte-ordered fields; no Unicode normalization. Values retain numeric text. Unknown lexical fields survive; profile permissions are separate. Explicit line versions; unsupported versions abstain. Escapes are backslash, quote and newline. Canonical lines exclude terminal LF; collection framing is separate. Explicit absence is rejected for signing.\n\nThe published #bound:1 profile is unchanged: class, algorithm, digest, blob and source are required, in that order, with exact encodeURIComponent round trip. No optional location is added to this profile.\n" at=witness:topos023-canonical-bytes by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/canonical-bytes shape=docs/contracts/canonical-bytes.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Consumed algebra alignment\n\nTopos prepares an exact Obligations 0.4.7 selection. The retained focused comparison passes 28 cases on each of 0.4.6 and 0.4.7 with the same diagnostic snapshot. It covers the consumed projection, incompatibility, refinement, withdrawal, effective order, standing, identity and wire behavior. This is not equivalence of every theorem or API.\n\nThe supplemental reader seals the selected manifest/lock, consumed source and captured comparison. It checks this evidence without running a second algebra or granting authority. Historical 0.2.3 installed-package and consumed-algebra captures remain historical; they are not relabeled as new installations.\n\nThe published 0.4.7 region-measure and key-lineage contracts retain their documentary status contradiction. It is recorded, not silently corrected or treated as a new proof. Stochastic T2 and physical-machine portability remain outside this interpretation contract.\n\nAdmission, public delivery and a stable consumer pin require their own receipts and exact bytes; this document does not establish them.\n" at=witness:contextual-reading-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/algebra-alignment shape=docs/contracts/algebra-alignment.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Consumed algebra compatibility\n\nTopos 0.2.3 consumes published Obligations 0.4.6: npm archive `sha256:077524d0fb48d4b9ba4fcb308e27b29784dc2591f25f601c8afd1f173ec48b04`, 588,259 bytes and 471 members. Its 166 compiled algebra members match 0.4.5. The compatibility comparison uses published Topos 0.2.2 code and the algebra surface it actually consumes; it does not establish equivalence of every theorem or API.\n\nThe bounded interval parse/meet/emit comparison ran in the reservoir and shipping places against 0.4.5 and 0.4.6: eight executions. The portable reader published with Topos 0.2.2 supplies the four-case 0.4.6 quartet on those places. Positive projections are `1..1`; negatives refuse with `REFUSE·consumed-algebra differing expected projection`. Input bytes, reader, selected Topos and Obligations modules, and actual outputs are identified by digest. Two places under one origin are not independent origins.\n\nThe conformance reader binds this contract, the selected manifest, four public samples and the native capture vector. Missing or altered inputs and undeclared readings refuse. A documentation correction changes its explicit file bindings; it does not replace the existing mathematical assertions or increase reader limits.\n\nThis evidence does not certify the other profiles, grant authority, verify public asset availability or approve a release archive. Conditional lineage identification, causal subgroup recovery and oracle-free reference remain open research demands; the empirical result remains unmeasured.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/consumed-algebra shape=docs/contracts/consumed-algebra.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Contextual reading witness\n\n`topos-reading-witness/1` represents a reading, its context and references to evidence. It is a candidate public form; parsing does not authenticate, authorize, execute or admit a reading.\n\nAll fields are nonempty strings without whitespace/control characters. Digests use lowercase `sha256:` plus 64 hexadecimal digits. The class is `attest`. Required digests identify the reading, contract, model, parameters, inputs, selection, scale, resolution, reader, implementation, context, result and evidence. `unit` names the measurement unit; `run` names the producer execution. The digests identify exact referenced bytes; the host must supply and verify those bytes and their bindings. The contract, parameter, scale and resolution records remain distinct: writing their names does not establish a composition law or infer independence.\n\nTwo explicit authentication profiles are represented:\n\n- `key-covered`: a named `key`, `coverage` digest and `evidence` digest. The consumer verifies the covering signature, admitted key and coverage under the actual selected protocol.\n- `ci-run-unkeyed`: repository, source commit identity, workflow digest, producer run and positive decimal attempt, plus the evidence digest. The consumer verifies those bindings and result bytes through its declared CI trust path. Neither a copied run URL nor a class label is proof. This profile does not invent a signature by a place key.\n\n`encodeReadingWitness` and `decodeReadingWitness` reuse canonical wire field encoding. They reject missing, extra, malformed or noncanonical fields. The canonical encoded witness is the complete identity preimage; the host hashes it using the declared algorithm. Property insertion order does not change these bytes. A changed input, context, result or proof reference changes the witness bytes.\n\n`readingWitnessStatementBytes` gives the canonical `topos-reading-statement/1` preimage containing every witness field except `evidence`. A covering proof must bind this statement according to its declared evidence protocol. Excluding the proof digest avoids a circular signature/hash. The complete witness includes that digest and therefore commits to the exact proof reference. Topos does not sign or verify this statement.\n\n`inspectReadingWitness` returns the canonical witness and statement bytes, `status=declared`, `authentication=not verified`, `authorization=not established`, and the outstanding checks: digest bindings, model/unit/scale/resolution, provenance and consuming policy. It never accepts a caller-supplied `verified` flag. A valid CI witness may still be rejected by the consuming operation's policy. Reuse preserves the original producer run; a later verifier cannot relabel it as newly produced evidence.\n\nThe samples are synthetic form vectors in two unrelated contexts; their digests deliberately identify no delivered proof. They pay schema/canonicality tests only. Native consumer verification of actual byte/provenance bindings, admission, receipts and public delivery remain separate obligations. No owner or private key is needed for this form test.\n" at=witness:contextual-reading-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/reading-witness shape=docs/contracts/reading-witness.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Contextual view of a consumer-produced authority judgment\n\nParsing is a declaration, not permission. authorityCardAnswer compares current consumer context and explicit clock before accepting a judgment through a receiver-selected verification port. It never calculates allow/ask/deny from grants.\n\nReuse: `normalizeRegistryHorizon`, `existing consumer authority evaluation`.\n\ncommits: place/snapshot, key/authority/coverage, policy/budget/demands, grants/retirements, catalog/standingContract, explicit horizon and issuedAt, request-digest decisions, external judgment evidence\n\nConsumer obligations:\n\n- Verify the actual judge, signature/coverage, every statement/context binding and accepted clock source.\n- Supply current authority identity after withdrawal, even when content snapshot is unchanged.\n\nCaller supplies byte and per-collection item budgets; no ambient clock, I/O, default verifier or universal region list.\n\nTwo receiver fixtures verify signature plus a fixed judgment statement. This is not a live production authority fold.\n\nContract meaning only. Admission, production consumer verification and public delivery require their separate acta.\n" at=witness:authority-and-governance-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/authority-card shape=docs/contracts/authority-card.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Cross-verification\n\nBound 0.2.5 archive sha256:e0765eeb54fcfec1c9c89a67094641957efead062ffd8a45900f2a3e3d56696b is the read-only producer. The exact published standing, receipts and refusal catalog, legacy reference and captured responses/CLI cases are replayed against current Topos members. Evidence is bounded to these bytes, fixtures and selected Node 22/24 executions in two places. Native captures certify this replay, not full adoption or a new Bound execution.\n\nTwo demands remain open: slash-bearing legacy names require explicit selection before the new CI profile; responsesOf frames responses without admitting their protocol version. Seven new profiles have no producer found in Bound 0.2.5. Neither absence nor framing parity proves compatibility outside this corpus. Public exports do not establish downstream adoption; exact-archive delivery remains separately verified. The three research cards stay open; empirical 1a remains unmeasured.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/cross-verification shape=docs/contracts/cross-verification.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Declared provider and host contracts\n\nProvider-owned standing inputs are selected by offer-relative claims and handed separately from place lines. They never grant the place authority. Missing required inputs refuse; disagreements retain their coordinates.\n\nA capsule may declare its region contract in standing-regions@1. Selection reads that descriptor without fetching or executing its artifact. A requested artifact must reproduce the declared regions, including their reads and roles.\n\nReader limits and input presence are declared policies. A completed empty answer differs from a missing required input; bounded-process@1 refuses incomplete, oversized or timed-out execution.\n\nContent-request/1 asks a host mechanism for already pinned immutable bytes. Sources name locations; digests name content. The host verifies bytes before storage. Mutable observation and authenticated release lineage require separate contracts.\n\n\n## Whole-region walk\n\nThe whole-region@1 contract reads its resolution labels from wire/walk/resolutions and their projections from wire/walk/projections. Labels are nonnegative discrete coordinates, not hardcoded levels or a measure of information. This profile offers inventory (region identities), summary (digests and exact counts for differences), and history (complete requested regions that differ). The reference declaration maps @0 to inventory, @1 to summary and @8 to history; a place can declare @4, @6 or further labels without changing code. Every label needs exactly one declared projection; an undeclared label refuses rather than inventing detail. A digest is not a proof for an arbitrary slice or an unseen suffix. Verified regional prefixes retain all earlier records and reject insertion at already verified epochs.\n\nTwo clocks remain separate. Original signed records keep their origin-ledger epoch. They are evidence, never receiver authority or key coverage. The receiver creates its own signed import receipt through its declared signer, at its next local epoch. Evidence and receipt must become visible in one transaction; an interrupted pre-publication import admits neither. Exact replay retains one receipt and adds no lines or origins.\n\nWithdrawals resolve against exact IDs within the same origin's authenticated history. They never target local lines merely sharing a scope or ID. Keys, snapshots and delivery copies do not establish independent object origins. The selected form/provider contract still decides attribution and independence; no extra meet or state is computed here.\n\nwalkSelection reads the admitted wire/walk declarations. walkSnapshot reuses native receipt projection for exchange history, separate from semantic cell-region receipt identity. walkAt selects the requested resolution without transport. walkHeaders returns unsigned statements for the declared signer. readWalkHeaders checks authenticated framing. readForeignWalk verifies whole-region commitments and earlier prefixes against the selected origin contract, returning verified foreign evidence and an unsigned native-format import-receipt proposal. It never signs, lands, grants coverage or advances a clock.\n\nThe receiver authenticates the declared sender context, historical wire and provider before calling the SDK. The host verifies and signs the local proposal, then atomically publishes the foreign evidence, sender metadata and signed receiver receipt. Delivered payload bytes, protocol bytes and newly committed bytes are distinct measurements.\n\n\n## Origin and authority inputs\n\nwalkOrigin reads exactly one declared walk/origin. It is the ledger attribution, not a key, snapshot or automatically independent object origin. walkAuthority reads explicit public keys, classes, coverage and resolution from a pinned origin context; journal identities without public keys are not signers. Sender signatures authenticate history under that snapshot and grant no receiver authority. Payload key declarations cannot replace the pinned context. Context rotation requires a separately declared lineage; the native profile refuses a changed context for an already received origin.\n\nwalkSnapshot may qualify regions with the declared origin. walkInventory joins verified region commitments, retaining distinct origin namespaces, merging identical region commitments idempotently, and refusing different digests or counts for one coordinate. A receiver announces already verified prefixes at @0; the sender still exports only its own history at @8. An acknowledged prefix transfers no semantic payload. Inventory commitments neither re-sign foreign history nor create independent origins.\n\n## Wire declaration disagreement\n\nConflicting live declarations of one wire alphabet refuse by its wire coordinate. A writer may supersede its own earlier alphabet at a newer epoch under the existing admission contract; independently retained writers must agree. An ambiguous fields or required list never becomes an empty alphabet.\n\n\n## Profile boundaries\n\nA resolution label does not count regions or create information. Inventory, summary and whole-history are the encodings implemented by this named exchange profile, not a universal alphabet of Topos offers. Without a partition declaration, historical whole-region@1 keeps its native receipt-family partition. The optional scope-coordinates@1 profile below refines exchange coordinates. It does not change semantic cell folds or their region contract.\n\nLedger origins are opaque identifiers. When projected into region coordinates their bytes are percent-encoded as one segment; slashes, spaces and percent signs cannot create a namespace collision. Context lineage, signatures, complete region commitments and origin-local exact withdrawals remain required. Numeric epochs, counts and resolution labels currently refuse values outside the implementation's exact integer range; no lossy conversion is accepted.\n\n\n## Declared coordinate refinement\n\nwire/walk/partition=scope-coordinates@1 selects complete signed history at each exact source scope as the exchange unit. Source scope segments and the opaque origin are escaped independently in receipt coordinates. New source coordinates create new units without a new alphabet or a fixed region count. This is a coordinate projection of authenticated history, not a filesystem traversal, a cell restsOn relation, or a new information measure.\n\nwire/walk/refinements maps every declared resolution label to a positive coordinate depth or all. A depth groups complete coordinate commitments by that declared source prefix. all exposes the exact source coordinates. Groups fold canonical child-coordinate digest and count claims using the native receipt projection; they are not separately stored trees or artifacts. The root remains the same complete-coordinate inventory root at every detail. Raising a numeric label has no implicit effect: the declared cut determines the answer.\n\nInventory and summary can show declared groups or exact coordinates. Summary acknowledges verified regional inventory, including foreign evidence under its own origin namespace. History requires all and carries complete differing coordinate histories; a grouped digest cannot admit a record fragment. An equal verified group covers its children; an equal verified set of exact children covers their group. Differing groups can be refined before requesting history, and verified unchanged children are reused. No provider, transport or key material is invoked to compute a cut. Metadata is signed only through the existing declared signer.\n\nThe sender context pins the history field and partition contract. The receiver decodes under that context, not its own partition or fields. Earlier verified complete-coordinate prefixes remain required, with original epochs and exact-origin withdrawals unchanged. A previously unasked coordinate is not claimed to have a verified earlier prefix. Changing an admitted origin context still requires separately declared lineage; refinement labels do not migrate history or rewrite its signatures.\n\nThe shipped reference sample declares cuts at 0, 1, 4, 6 and 8, with 4 showing an intermediate coordinate depth and 6 carrying complete histories. These are example labels and depths, not limits. Other Topos partition interpretations require their own declared contract; scope-coordinates@1 does not claim to implement them.\n\n## Typed cell, claim and mark records\n\nThe object profile is selected by admitted `wire/object/*` claims in a historical wire snapshot. The epoch of `wire/object/types` is its activation E; a typed record must have epoch >= E. An older record keeps the older grammar. Merely adding object words to `wire/fields` does not admit a typed profile.\n\n`fromLine` selects grammar before validating required fields: a record without `type` remains configuration, retaining its required `at`, `role`, `form`, `measure` and `value`; a typed record selects its exact required/allowed row. Configuration cannot carry object-only fields. An incomplete typed record receives no configuration defaults. The parse result distinguishes configuration from cell/claim/mark. `sign=+1|-1` is an object act, `sig` its signature envelope, and a host sign operation produces that envelope.\n\nThe common object envelope is `type`, `scope`, `id`, `epoch`, `by`, `sig`. Object records reject `at`: epoch orders acts, and `widens` names a local join witness. The following is the shipped reference profile. The admitted row alphabets, not this table or a host decoder, are the source of required/allowed field permission.\n\n| Row | Fields in addition to the common envelope | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| cell | form, measure, params, restsOn, topos | Define a cell and its selected form/context. |\n| claim/positive | sign, origin, value | Observe an encoded span, sign=+1. |\n| claim/negative | sign, takes | Withdraw an exact claim ID, sign=-1. |\n| mark/travelling | sign, pole, reach, value | Ceiling sign: sign=+1, pole=ceiling, reach=travels. |\n| mark/local | sign, pole, reach, value, widens | Local join: sign=+1, pole=ceiling, reach=local; another mark, retaining the travelling sign. |\n| mark/floor-travelling | sign, pole, reach, value | Travelling require: sign=+1, pole=floor, reach=travels; no widens. |\n| mark/negative | sign, takes | Withdraw an exact mark ID, sign=-1; no pole/reach/widens. |\n\nEach row has both `wire/object/required/<row>` and `wire/object/allowed/<row>`. Unknown keys, missing required keys, floor/local, floor+widens, and travelling widening refuse without coercion. A configuration demand is unrelated to a floor mark. `by` authenticates a signing key; it is not an independent observation origin.\n\nFor a cell, `restsOn=none` represents no parents; otherwise it is a canonical byte-ordered alphabet of coordinates. Several parents are permitted. Only declared cell-coordinate edges form object rest; a directory prefix, a configuration artifact digest, and a host source location do not. Context validation rejects reachable cycles before algebraic parts are requested.\n\nThe cell's `topos` names folded standing, and its `params` identifies declared parameter bytes. A selected provider decodes values and supplies origin/witness authorization from admitted evidence. A host does not infer interval endpoints, map a key to an origin, or accept widens as its own witness. Missing pin, artifact, decoder, origin or witness refuses by cause. Cell definitions and pins are immutable in this profile; migration is not inferred.\n\n`objectHistory` preserves typed history separately from configuration; `validateObjectContext` checks IDs, cell ownership, exact takes, witness order and declared rest under the selected context. Exact redelivery is idempotent; one ID with different canonical content refuses. Signature-envelope differences never bypass verification.\n\nAfter admission, the instrument projects the acts into Obligations. Sign and require travel along declared rest; a witnessed join is local. Descendants inherit travelling marks without local widening. Live exact-ID withdrawals remain confined to their authenticated history. Keys, snapshots, signing devices and repeated receipts do not create independent origins. Compatible meets are not forks. Object states and their precedence come from Obligations; configuration folds do not compute object encounters.\n\nThe package's `samples/walk/interval.json`, `interval.no.json`, `alphabet.json` and `alphabet.no.json` carry exact historical wire declarations and signed positive/negative exchange records. They are examples of declared profiles, not a production activation epoch or a promise that their keys authorize another place. Host-native object output remains a separate product vector.\n\n## Demands, evidence and declared dependencies\n\n`@lapxo/topos/contract` exports `programsOf`, `programInputs` and `renderPrograms`. These project explicit program coordinates, their `needs`, their `restsOn` predecessors and admitted evidence. They do not admit lines, execute work or compute cell states.\n\n`programsOf({programs, evidence, minimum, elapsed?, ceiling?})` takes programs with `scope`, `needs` and `restsOn`, plus an admitted evidence map. Evidence labels are `met`, `unmet`, `unread` and `refused`. The caller verifies evidence before passing it. A file at a requirement's coordinate does not establish its meaning or conformance.\n\nA missing proof remains unread. An empty requirement is vacuous and never completes a program. An absent minimum refuses. Unknown coordinates, duplicate declarations and dependency cycles refuse by coordinate. An unmet predecessor blocks successors. `next` retains all eligible unresolved programs; presentation order cannot authorize a successor. Only explicitly optional programs carry over after a measured deadline; no version arithmetic or stage numbers are inferred. An absent elapsed reading keeps the deadline unread.\n\nThe native `admitted-demands@1` view profile reads its selectors from the selected provider's standing projection, separately from place lines. `wire/programs/{selection,minimum,evidence,elapsed,ceiling}` each names a coordinate or handed evidence region; `wire/programs/profile` names the profile. The selected demand lines provide their own `needs` and `restsOn`. Evidence statuses come from the declared region's receipts. Elapsed time also comes from that region, as an exact finite interval; a configured elapsed value is not an observation. The ceiling is a declared interval, and both measurements must use the same unit. `restsOn` here names program predecessors, never cell rest or artifact bytes.\n\nA place chooses its view names and the offered regions. The instrument's historical `programs` summary need not be replaced: a declared `view/programs` may select this provider's demand projection. The provider performs no filesystem, network, signing or admission work. Its output names demands, prerequisites, missing or unread evidence and eligible next coordinates. None of these labels introduces another Obligatory state.\n\n`samples/programs/yes.json` supplies two unrelated plans. `samples/programs/no.json` supplies ambiguous or cyclic inputs. `samples/programs/cli.vector.json` captures native programs/card requests in fresh isolated places, including unread evidence and named cycle refusals. These captures are evidence for that candidate reader and those provider bytes; they are not receipts for another place's work.\n\n## Publication coverage\n\n`@lapxo/topos/contract` also exports `publicationCoverage(rows, artifacts, proofs, digest)`. Each selected public coordinate declares members with the roles `contract`, `yes`, `vector` and `no`, each by digest. The artifact map supplies the corresponding bytes; the supplied digest implementation verifies them. A changed or corrupt member refuses by coordinate.\n\nProofs are conformance readings already admitted by the caller. Each proof records its exact quartet of input identities and its status. Stale input identities refuse. Four available members without an admitted proof remain unread; a refused or unmet proof cannot close the coordinate. Empty surface selection is vacuous and refuses. The result reports every unresolved coordinate and a missing count. This projection cannot create a receipt, run a vector or authenticate the observer. Those operations belong to the selected measurer and host admission boundary.\n\nSupported operations and outstanding capability demands must be inventoried separately. A demand does not become an exported implementation because its name appears in a lock. Removing public support requires its explicit compatibility decision; missing documentation cannot be hidden by renaming or excluding an implemented surface.\n## Public act results\n\nAn act preserves canonical signed records in effective order, including their signer and origin clock; actBytes excludes only sig. actIdentity additionally binds the receiving coordinate, declared result context, local commit epoch and logical placements. These are act identities, not Topos standing pins. Placement coordinates are relative logical names; host paths never enter this identity.\n\nactResultOf verifies records and the native digest receipt through separate callbacks. Record authentication and coverage do not follow from the receipt. local-act@1 is explicitly selected by wire/act-result, wire/act-result-scope and wire/act-result-context; absent selection retains the historical path. Conflicting or incomplete selections refuse. The host publishes the original records, routing and receipt atomically. Replay returns that committed pair without advancing the clock. An error after commit does not establish rollback.\n\nHistorical inspection uses the authority, digest alphabet, native receipt identity and result profile admitted before that act. Retiring a key or changing a profile does not erase authenticated history, and inspecting history does not authorize a new act or claim conformance. A missing or corrupt result refuses by identity.\n\n## Current reader policy and retained evidence\n\nReaderReference separates the declared reader id from its executable module. bounded-process@1 retains its module-coordinate and upper-ceiling semantics. bounded-process@2 meets the global and declared-reader interval limits through Obligatory; an empty meet refuses by reader and limit. No arbitrary default reader or profile is invented.\n\nThe host validates the effective policy before cache lookup. Current-response identity includes those execution limits alongside implementation and complete inputs. A changed effective policy reopens the reading; unchanged policy and inputs reuse its verified response. This applies to direct readers, capsule answers and independent regional reading receipts. Retained evidence is history, not unconditional permission to answer today. Preview does not execute to fill a missing response. Operational failure, interruption and partial output are never successful receipts.\n\nThe shipped samples/public-boundary/yes.json and no.json contain two unrelated signed fixture lots, a correctly bound receipt and named rejected changes. vectors/public-boundary.json captures the SDK result projection and lifetime verdicts. The fixture signatures are not committed Bound receipts or release acceptance. Consumers verify record authority and native receipt provenance through the declared boundary.\n\n\n## Reading contracts (0.1.13)\n\nThe functions below are pure projections. They read supplied, admitted inputs; they do not fetch, execute, sign, land or create receipts. Their conformance inputs are samples/readings/yes.json and no.json, with the reviewed SDK vector vectors/readings.json. Those outputs are not CLI admission or host conformance.\n\n### Semantic standing\n\nsemanticStandingBytes and readSemanticStanding implement semantic-standing@1: canonical live claims in effective order, excluding sig, by, epoch, expires, repo and shape. Re-delivery adds no inscription. Sorting sign and join would identify distinct acts; reversing their effective order changes these bytes. The historical standingBytes/readStanding set encoding stays unchanged. A host must select the new profile explicitly before resolving a place by its projection. Archives and JSON manifests are not semantic standing.\n\n### Readings and evidence\n\nunionReadings retains every world's reading with attribution. World-order permutation and exact replay preserve the union; an empty reader cannot suppress another. Alternative value encodings are named by coordinate; the selected form interprets compatibility, and Obligations computes object states. Worlds, keys, snapshots and copies do not create independent origins. renderWriter requires one declared writer for a file, even if two writers propose identical output.\n\nfoldedEvidence receives historical classification and the instrument's existing fold. Local authenticated history, foreign evidence and unadmitted inscriptions remain distinct. The callback cannot manufacture an authenticated inscription. Supersessions and withdrawals are applied by the existing fold; withdrawal markers remain history and are excluded from the projected standing. This projection never supplies receiver coverage or replaces its authority lock.\n\n### Sources and snapshots\n\noriginRegion distinguishes an explicit origin-backed region from coordinate and leaf regions. Conflicting source projections refuse by coordinate. The host resolves the declared source and transport; selecting a descriptor initiates no retrieval or execution.\n\nsnapshotIdentity commits source, content digest, observation under a declared clock, optional declared freshness and interpreter identity. It is neither a standing pin nor an independent origin. Missing freshness remains unknown. responseIdentity binds that snapshot, principal, authorization, request and implementation. snapshotAccess reads separate folded permissions for historical inspection and current reuse; unknown freshness or a changed account, grant, request, snapshot or implementation prevents current reuse. These functions cannot authenticate an observation or mint its receipt. The host must verify bytes and commit complete authenticated evidence atomically.\n\n### Policies\n\ncomposePolicy requires a selected field interpretation: meet, exact, or explicitly own. Obligations supplies meet and order; an empty meet stays empty. Own values never bypass inherited meet constraints. Conflicting exact or own encodings remain incompatible. policyTransition distinguishes compatibility, narrowing, empty intersection, widening-needs-authority and non-meet fields. A widening verdict grants no authority; the existing witness and coverage boundary remains required.\n\n### Command port\n\nThe explicit command-lot@1 profile carries opaque UTF-8 request strings in one framed invocation per lot. checkCommandPort requires an admitted declaration coordinate under port/, its coverage, verified executable closure, positive exact time/byte bounds, explicit environment names and stderr policy. A URL, standing or program output cannot register an executable. commandRequest frames the declared coordinate, implementation and lot identity. commandResponse binds the complete response to them, checks byte limits and every response member, and rejects interruption, nonzero status, forbidden stderr and incomplete output. The host invokes without a shell, forwards only the declared environment, applies time and cancellation limits, and commits durable evidence after complete validation. The SDK does none of that execution. The existing signing-lot/1 contract stays unchanged; adapting it to this host port does not change signed bytes or verification.\n\n### Inspection and lineage\n\nreleaseSuccessors reads authenticated predecessor links, keeps alternate successors and rejects conflicts or cycles; it never chooses newest by URL. inspectionSelection names existing admitted coordinates for keep/follow without fetching or granting authority. Actual materialization and bump remain host views, not new verbs. weightOf reports verified bytes by digest: absent or incomplete scans remain unknown, copies count once, conflicting sizes refuse. vacuityOf reads a declared complete epoch window; no observation is neither correctness nor another object state.\n\n### Compatibility\n\nCanonical UTF-8 field and set order, contextual none and named refusal for incompatible live wire declarations retain their existing meaning. New order-sensitive place projection does not reinterpret old Topos pins. No operation above changes REQUIRED, FREE, CONFLICT or FORBIDDEN.\n\n\n## Semantic shadow and compatibility\n\nThe opt-in semantic-standing@1 profile projects already folded live lines in effective order, without the delivery envelope. The instrument supplies that order; the SDK neither authenticates nor folds history. wire/identity/{profile,form,shadow,projections} declares the form, shadow resolution and projection labels. A commitment is <form>@<shadow-resolution>:<algorithm:hex>, computed from the canonical UTF-8 shadow bytes. Undeclared labels refuse. Resolution labels are exact text, not a fixed list or machine-number range.\n\nThe existing set standing format retains its meaning. vectors/canonical-compatibility.json records key order, spacing, literal number text, Unicode, quoted none and byte/text distinctions without changing that meaning. Form interpretation is separate from wire canonicalization.\n\n## Ordered cell receipt inputs\n\nsemantic-live@1 retains the published sorted-set input encoding. It cannot distinguish a sign followed by a local join from the reverse order. semantic-live@2 is an explicit opt-in: live own marks retain effective act order; travelling ancestor marks exclude local widenings; claims, rest coordinates, selected standing and implementation inputs remain part of identity. Signature and delivery fields are excluded. The host must preserve the ordered input sequence when hashing it and bind the selected profile.\n\nThe native counterexample is captured in vectors/order.cli.json: sign then join yields signed 0..100, join then sign yields signed 0..50, with distinct receipts and idle unchanged folds. These are source-candidate CLI captures, not installed release acceptance.\n\n## Pure interpretation entry\n\n@lapxo/topos/core exposes pure wire, projection and declared-contract functions. Storage, authentication, execution and transport remain host work. Its transitive runtime imports are checked with the compiler syntax tree; the declaration of this entry does not certify native release closure.\n\n\n## World adoption and release discovery\n\nA world is a place selected by its canonical standing commitment; sources names locations and uses names identity. Required artifacts and files follow the selected standing's declared regions and restsOn closure. Delivery archives are not standing identity. The semantic-standing@1 profile is opt-in; existing published set pins are not rewritten as new projection identities.\n\nUntil authenticated lineage discovery is available, a world release delivers release.json with standing and blob digests; predecessor is optional delivery metadata. readReleaseDelivery reads only that declared release-delivery@1 record. It does not authenticate lineage, verify content, grant authority or bless a URL. The host verifies downloaded standing and every named artifact. releaseSuccessors accepts authenticated predecessor links and retains alternative successors; it never selects newest by URL. A Bound bump or needs proposal must carry the selected standing digest from that verified lineage, and a person signs the proposed local act. Those host views require their own native acceptance before being advertised.\n\nA selected standing may declare wire/adoption/profile=place-requirements@1. Its needs/place/<coordinate> demands use measure=line and value containing one canonical semantic place declaration. placeRequirements returns the requesting world's digest, requirement coordinate and desired line. Identical deliveries add no authority; differing requirements remain separate. Borrowed signatures, signer ids, epochs and withdrawals refuse. Absence declares no requirements; an undeclared requirements profile refuses. No wire field is added.\n\nThe host repair view compares these requirements with the place before adoption, checks its grammar, conflicts and coverage, and proposes missing declarations as an unsigned local lot. It never copies world history into the place or signs on the person's behalf. Prose separators, region-measures and other required place lines belong to the world's declaration rather than a consumer-specific prelude. A requirement is not a paid demand or an authorized act.\n\nThe SDK samples cover two unrelated places and missing-profile/borrowed-history refusals. Native repair, one-pin acquisition, bump and release-asset delivery remain host/world acceptance work, not claims established by these pure SDK readers.\n\n\n## Native SDK conformance\n\nWire fields are interpreted using the declared historical wire. The positive sample retains fields from their own era; the negative sample requires an undeclared field and refuses by name. The native captures include both read and refused lines, on two places.\n\nEvery offered form delegates its lattice and generator to Obligatory. The native vector checks the lattice axioms and encoding roundtrip on the declared generated points. Broken meet, reversed order and a missing top law are separate counterexamples; the negative sample exposes the missing top law. This is sampled conformance, not a proof over unsampled points.\n\nA capsule answers only a declared region and only the reads it is handed. The positive sample projects a declared input while excluding a private line. The negative sample withholds a required read; an unoffered region also refuses. Receipt admission is a verified publication reading of these samples and the captured instrument output, not a declaration paying itself.\n\nThe shared native readings vector carries the three captures under named audit coordinates. Each quartet is retained separately by its implementation and handed input digests. Missing inputs or a captured output disagreement refuse by coordinate.\n\nThe historical SDK agent-effects ceiling is retired as inert here: the SDK interprets declarations and framing but performs no network act. Network-effect observation and authorization remain host responsibilities; this retirement does not declare zero network acts or relax a host ceiling.\n\n\n## Reading context and semantic receipt names\n\nThe opt-in reading-context@1 profile names a reading from the admitted instrument identity, complete executable implementation, interpreted policy, complete transitive inputs and explicitly declared relevant environment. readingIdentityBytes emits a domain-separated UTF-8 JSON tuple; readingIdentity applies the caller-supplied admitted digest. Coordinate/reference pairs sort by the existing UTF-8 order. Missing collections remain unknown and refuse; explicit empty collections remain explicit. Duplicate coordinates and undeclared context fields refuse by name. References remain opaque; these pure operations cannot verify a closure, authenticate a reader or authorize reuse.\n\nThe reading-receipt@1 profile commits the reading identity and complete result identity. Distinct results keep distinct receipt names even for the same reading. The result identity includes the complete outcome, including refusal, interruption or failure; none is a successful receipt merely because it has a name. Time, signatures and execution diagnostics stay in an authenticated delivery envelope. No receipt is created or stored by the SDK. Current responses also commit the existing responseIdentity context as a named input and must pass snapshotAccess at reuse time; naming a retained receipt never bypasses principal, grants, snapshot, freshness or current permissions.\n\nSamples/readings/reading-identity.yes.json and reading-identity.no.json cover two unrelated contexts and named refusals. vectors/reading-identity.json fixes language-independent canonical bytes. vectors/native/reading-identity.cli.json records published Bound 0.1.8 output and replay on two fixture-authorized places. These are SDK conformance results, not acceptance of Bound caching or lane scheduling. No field is added to the wire, no standing format is reinterpreted, and no object state changes.\n\n\nThe wire-conformance@1 reference reader delegates to the maintained fromLine parser. It observes declared JSON cases and retains input, explanation and source kind. A matched negative case is conformance evidence, never admission of the rejected line. The previous bundled stdin sample and its process captures are withdrawn with their memberships; this replacement invokes no subprocess.\n\n\n## Admitted place inputs\n\nThe opt-in admitted-place-inputs@1 profile selects consumer evidence without changing provider-owned standing inputs. A world declares wire/place-inputs role=writes measure=id value=admitted-place-inputs@1 and offers/<offer>/place-inputs/<region> measure=reads, role=reads for optional input or role=demands for required input, with scope masks in value. Required absence refuses by selector name; optional absence stays empty and pays no demand.\n\nplaceInputs(world, offer, region, live, hash) is pure: the host must supply an authenticated, already folded live canonical consumer shadow in effective order. The function neither verifies authority nor folds raw history. It reuses semantic-standing@1 envelope exclusion, removes exact redelivery, retains effective order and conflicting live claims, and rejects withdrawal history. The selector never chooses a winning claim.\n\nIdentity is canonical UTF-8 JSON [profile, ordered semantic selectors, ordered selected live claims], followed by newline and hashed by the declared digest function. An unrelated live claim does not change this identity. withPlaceInputs appends the projection digest to reading-context@1 inputs without dropping instrument, implementation, policy, transitive inputs, environment or current-answer references; duplicate coordinates refuse.\n\nRequest and Asked carry optional place: PlaceInputs, distinct from provider, lines and regional receipts. JSON framing and render/receipt shells preserve this field. An absent field preserves legacy delivery. This profile conveys evidence, never grants authority or proves independence. SDK framing does not itself activate a host handoff; the consuming host must establish its authenticated boundary and identity.\n\nThe profile is proved by two distinct sample places through the installed instrument, with captured projections, unchanged replay, and named missing-input/history refusals. Both places are on one declared macOS machine; cross-machine evidence remains pending under Bound L6.\n\n\n## Prospective place-act context\n\nThis isolated prototype proposes place-act-context@1; it is not activated by the published package. local-act@1 retains its exact format and meaning. Original signed records keep their signer, scope, bytes and origin epoch. Each root-admitted logical place (` . ` or `<place>/TARGET.bound`) has its own receipt clock; origin namespace is authenticated separately from placement.\n\nOriginal signature verification precedes existing key eligibility. The shared authority-at-epoch primitive uses the explicitly authenticated root authority epoch and qualified coverage coordinate; it never replaces a signed epoch. A grant starting at root epoch 5 can validate an unchanged local epoch 2 record at root authority epoch 6. Every original signer authenticates the complete origin frame. Missing or borrowed proofs, namespace swaps, unknown clocks and partial receiver signatures refuse. Neither a snapshot, name nor retained signature grants fresh coverage or independence.\n\nCanonical profile tuples use UTF-8 JSON arrays, exact strings and decimal-text safe-integer clocks. Byte, record, place, policy, proof and signer resources are mandatory. Bytes are bounded before parse, counts before signature/hash work; all public signing constructors and standalone verification enforce the same boundary. No wire field is added.\n\nHistorical inspection and replay use the authenticated committed prefix; they do not revive revoked grants. Pure verification returns dependency references. The host must hold authority/policy protection through atomic publication, compare the selected previous heads and next epochs, and retain per-place signed receipts. The pure SDK does not prove storage freshness, crash recovery or power-loss durability.\n\nLegacy globally-clocked histories retain their root namespace, signatures, epochs and withdrawals. A host must not compare legacy epoch 22 with child epoch 2 using raw maximum or apply a child withdrawal across origins. The new profile requires its own admitted compatibility and host projection boundary.\n\nThe exact proposed transaction tuple is `[\"place-act-context@1\",\"transaction\",[\"authorityNamespace\",\"rootSnapshotReference\",\"rootAuthorityEpoch\",\"rootPrefixReference\",\"effectiveAuthorityHead\",[\"effective policy references sorted by UTF-8 bytes\"]],[[\"receivingNamespace\",\"previousHead\",\"nextEpoch\",\"originPrefixReference\",[\"ordered original signed lines\"],[[\"originProofSigner\",\"signatureValue\"]]]]]`. The origin and receipt tuples are `[\"place-act-context@1\",\"origin\",\"originProofSigner\",\"receivingNamespace\",\"originEpoch\",\"originPrefixReference\",\"rootSnapshotReference\",[\"original canonical signed lines in effective order\"]]` and `[\"place-act-context@1\",\"receipt\",\"receiptSigner\",\"receiverStatementScope\",\"transactionIdentity\",\"receivingNamespace\",\"previousHead\",\"nextEpoch\",\"rootSnapshotReference\"]`. Instrument-selected verification and namespace admission derive from the same authenticated root snapshot; packet-selected verifiers are not accepted.\n\nThese native samples measure the pure candidate API through installed Bound 0.1.8 on two places of one Mac. They do not enable Bound place-local transactions or pay L3. Other-machine evidence remains pending L6.\n\n## Public authority eligibility at an explicit clock\n\n`@lapxo/topos/wire` exports the existing pure `authorityAtEpoch(fields, signer, epoch, coverageScope, clockKind)` alongside `authorityOf`, `signersOf` and `windowAt`. It evaluates eligibility only: the host must first authenticate the original signed bytes, the signer, the selected authority snapshot and its clock. Calling it never authenticates a record or grants execution.\n\n`clockKind=\"authority\"` requires a known nonnegative integral epoch. Current windows, temporal coverage, depth and key class are checked at that explicit authority epoch; `coverageScope` is the declared authorization coordinate. The original signed scope, epoch and signature remain unchanged. `authorityOf` continues to use the original record epoch. A retired or uncovered key can therefore leave an authenticated historical record admitted while refusing a new action now. Unknown clocks refuse by name; the method does not invent independence or alter cell algebra.\n\nThe two native fixture datasets exercise a finite closed window and temporal coverage revocation, with actual disposable signatures and unchanged original bytes. Native no-samples preserve the public refusal; the installed public export, not an internal implementation path, is called. These are content/eligibility proofs on one Mac, not an execution sandbox or L6 portability payment.\n\n# Object preparation: effective cell-before-use order\n\nScope: object-context validation after historical grammar and authentication supplied by the caller. This contract does not authenticate transport order or authorize an object signature.\n\nThe proposed history has already passed historical wire grammar. Epoch is the numeric declared epoch. Effective order is ascending epoch, preserving the authenticated supplied sequence within an equal epoch. This function does not reconstruct transport history or sort equal-epoch records by ID, record family, digest, or scope.\n\nContext validation authenticates each received record before deduplication, and keeps the first occurrence of each identical signed ID. Nonidentical signed content for one ID refuses. These existing rules remain unchanged.\n\nA claim or mark requires its own scope's cell to precede it in effective order. A lower-epoch cell precedes even when its record arrived later. At the same epoch the cell must precede the act in the supplied effective sequence. Otherwise validation returns REFUSE with `object context: cell not declared before <act-id>` before projection. This applies to positive and withdrawing acts. Existing exact-target withdrawal, codec, origin, witness, coverage, cycle, and noncommuting-ceiling checks remain intact.\n\nAn arrival permutation is acceptable only when preparation reconstructs the same authenticated effective sequence. Swapping equal-epoch records in the sequence passed to validation changes effective order and carries no equivalence promise. A transport implementation must establish that sequence or refuse; this candidate makes no new transport claim.\n\nYes cases: interval and ladder cells preceding a same-epoch claim project REQUIRED. A later-epoch claim preceding the older cell in input still projects REQUIRED through the existing epoch ordering. Exact duplicate records retain first-occurrence order and deduplicate.\n\nNo cases: a same-epoch claim or ceiling mark preceding its cell refuses with the act ID. Nonidentical duplicate IDs retain the collision refusal. The historical sign/join noncommutativity vector remains unchanged.\n\nNo algebra, wire field, pin, coverage or ceiling change. SDK publication precedes final Bound consumption. This contract is exercised by interval and ladder sample places. The named no-sample is `object context: cell not declared before claim-A`. Contract publication and activation remain explicit SDK admission acts.\n" at=witness:effective-order-before-object-projection by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contracts shape=docs/contracts.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Distinguish attributed source, authenticated covering proof and written refusal\n\nA named admitted key and unsigned origin line do not authenticate an author. A covering proof binds the canonical origin statement; its own evidence digest is excluded to avoid self-reference. Authenticated attribution is not authorization. A refusal names producer, code, request digest, scope, context and next action; decoding does not authenticate its producer.\n\nReuse: `existing canonical wire`, `receiver-selected coverage verifier`.\n\nConsumer obligations:\n\n- Actual key admission and signature/coverage under the selected context.\n- Real key-material-request refusal is emitted by its consumer; Topos represents it and never owns the private signer.\n\nCaller supplies byte and per-collection item budgets; no ambient clock, I/O, default verifier or universal region list.\n\nTwo fixtures verify origin covering signatures with receiver-pinned public keys; sample refusal codes are attributed fixture declarations, not an admission or hijack verdict.\n\nContract meaning only. Admission, production consumer verification and public delivery require their separate acta.\n" at=witness:authority-and-governance-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/origin-refusal shape=docs/contracts/origin-refusal.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Finite deterministic projection chains\n\nCurrent aligned candidate contract: `topos-projection-chain/1`. The machine-readable source is [projection-chain.json](projection-chain.json). This profile is separate from M0.\n\n`measureProjectionChain` consumes one named finite domain, two total deterministic maps, one prior and a numerical/work policy. It delegates all entropy and exact probability arithmetic to Obligations 0.4.7 `mixProjectionLoss`. Its first result supplies the exact pushforward prior for the second link. An optional direct projection must equal the composition. Arbitrary pairs of projections are refused; a separately chosen downstream prior is refused; randomized maps are refused.\n\nA missing prior or exhausted declared-configurations budget returns `unmeasured`. A measured result names the model, mathematical contract digest and consumed function module digest; these identify bytes and do not prove correctness. The complete reading identity must also commit to the implementation and its transitive inputs. Results include all three conditional losses and the unrounded numerical residual. Tests compare that residual against the mathematical vector's declared binary64 tolerance of 1e-12, not an exact entropy claim.\n\nInput order of named atoms and map rows does not change the prepared model. Zero-mass fibers are retained with the exact probabilities supplied by Obligations. No independence assumption is introduced. Evaluation establishes this finite instance, not a general Lean theorem.\n\nThe source tests exercise reservoir and shipping namespaces, composition rejection, changed prior, randomized maps, missing prior, exhausted budget and zero-mass support. These are focused API vectors. The native instrument captured both positive places and rejected each incompatible direct projection. The supplemental reader verifies those captured outputs against the same implementation and seals its declared inputs. The prior 0.4.6 selection was admitted locally at epochs 22 and 23 and closed 29 native readings without opening a region. That retained evidence is not rerun or replaced by this alignment. The new dependency identity, reconciled metadata and any affected reader bindings still require their own exact selection and admission; 0.2.4 public delivery remains pending. These vectors do not authorize a release or stable Bound pin.\n\nThe 0.4.6 to 0.4.7 comparison preserves the mathematical contract and `mixProjectionLoss` module byte for byte. The focused consumer vector covers interval composition and conflict, narrowing and widening, coverage boundaries, withdrawal, canonical and effective standing, reading input identity, wire, walk refinement and finite projection chains. It passes 28 tests per version with identical diagnostic snapshot bytes. This bounds the evidence to the actual exercised surface; it is not a claim that package versions have identical full semantics. Receipt reuse remains subject to the complete selected input, implementation and environment identity.\n" at=witness:contextual-reading-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/projection-chain shape=docs/contracts/projection-chain.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Group, member and decision forms with declared k-of-n and prior-authority binding\n\nGroup names a set of regions and a snapshot. Member references a group by digest, named key, validity horizon and evidence. Decision references prior group, separate membership snapshot, exact proposal, context, threshold, horizon and proof references. Duplicate proof keys and impossible declared thresholds refuse structurally; proofs are not counted as verified votes.\n\nReuse: `normalizeRegistryQuorum`, `normalizeRegistryHorizon`, `existing named-threshold@1 syntax`.\n\nidentityBoundary: The group does not contain hashes of members that hash the group again. Membership is a separately named snapshot. A next-group proposal excludes a backlink to its authorizing decision. The decision names prior-group and next-proposal digests.\n\nConsumer obligations:\n\n- Native consumer resolves bytes, admitted member bindings, valid distinct signatures and eligibility at the declared deadline.\n- Group change is authorized under the prior group, never the proposed group.\n- Native consumer retains comparable admitted incompatible decisions as a named fork; opposed votes, corrupt bytes and distinct unverified declarations do not establish that fork.\n- If native threshold admission is absent, keep governance/native-threshold-admission open.\n\nCaller supplies byte and per-collection item budgets; no ambient clock, I/O, default verifier or universal region list.\n\nTwo places demonstrate declared predecessor binding and reject self-authorization. Actual quorum execution, admission and independence remain not established.\n\nContract meaning only. Admission, production consumer verification and public delivery require their separate acta.\n" at=witness:authority-and-governance-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/group-decision shape=docs/contracts/group-decision.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Installed archive acceptance\n\nBind recorded installation, public API/types and native closure of an exact archive to the interpreted source/type identities.\n\n- The archive digest, size and member count identify the immutable pre-admission archive tested in two clean consumers.\n- Source/type/manifest/README/citation/public-lock identities are explicit; the current reading refuses any changed input.\n- Installation, API, type checking, README examples and native closure have separately captured outcomes and times.\n- Both consumers use verified published Obligations0.4.7; install scripts are disabled and no private store, ledger, CAS or source symlinks are copied.\n- 121 public values and61 types from29 module bindings and18 subpaths are checked through installed exports, with12 relevant API cases.\n- 42 native readings are retained with zero openings; their source histories are not relabeled as newly executed matrices.\n- Release-ready is refused: a changed post-admission archive requires exact member/installation verification before final approval.\n- These are committed observations, not proof of production authority, threshold admission, empirical calibration or physical-machine independence.\n\nThe four native vectors and their exact inputs are selected from the declared vector document. Its claim is verified-before-acceptance-admission; no final release approval is inferred.\n" at=witness:installed-archive-acceptance by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/installed-acceptance shape=docs/contracts/installed-acceptance.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Integrated package contract\n\nTopos 0.2.3 exposes seven profiles through `@lapxo/topos/contract`, with Obligations 0.4.6. Runtime exports, type declarations, manifest-selected vectors and normative documents must agree. Existing wire bytes and `#bound:1` remain unchanged. An installed-package check compares actual members and exercises public exports; historical preparation captures do not certify a successor archive.\n\nProvider standings and runtime archives are identified by digest and resolved through declared public sources. A draft release is preparation only: those URLs remain proposed until their assets are publicly downloadable and verify against the selected digests. Clean-consumer verification must not depend on the author's filesystem, ledger or content store. Changes to normative inputs require explicit reader rebinding and fresh affected receipts; other receipts are reused only under their complete identities.\n\nM0 measures describe declarations, not generic aggregation, projection chains or numerical certification. Slash-bearing CI witness names require explicit profile migration; response framing alone does not validate protocol versions. These compatibility conditions remain open for downstream adoption. Research demands and empirical unmeasured results remain unchanged. No transport, signature, authority or release approval is inferred from these forms.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/integrated-package shape=docs/contracts/integrated-package.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# M0 measure declarations\n\nProfile: topos-measure-m0/1. Only the binary M0 domains actually published by @lapxo/obligations0.4.6 are represented. No wire, M/3, M/4 or protocol change. Non-M0 models, network parameters, 2e-DG, generic projections and resource budgets are refused here. Generic aggregation and projection-chain forms remain separate work.\n\nThe profile interprets declarations and compares them; it does not calculate, authenticate or certify statistical results. Every accepted numerical result remains declared-not-recomputed, certification not established, with model premises, computation and observation/calibration provenance still requiring verification. A theorem about uniqueness does not check a supplied inverse. Obligations0.4.6 publishes no public M0 inverse or K_eff interval API; this profile does not invent one. Public cofailurePairs supplies pair rates, not an interval or calibration receipt. coincidenceExcess requires truth and is not the oracle-free operation.\n\nNumerical declarations require model id, selected object, observation design, units, all applicable parameters, binary64 tolerance and result. Joint independence is an explicit scoped model hypothesis with provenance; names, shares or pairwise correlation never establish it. Weights are uniform with declared K or finite nonnegative normalized weights; zeros do not count as active support. Normalization tolerance is explicit, without silently rescaling weights. Unknown K is allowed only as the target of the declared identification question.\n\nPopulation moments select R2 and R4 under conditional iid with-replacement draws. They do not describe the full observable law, majority error or causal identification. Uniform-family identification requires externally known p in(0,1), c in(0,1], exact population expectations and unknown positive integer K. For general weights, only the compatible set defined by R2/R4 is represented; no singleton inverse. Published nonuniform collision at p=3/4, c=7/10,K_eff=147/4 versus c=4/5,K_eff=48 is preserved. R3 is redundant; separation by R6 for that pair proves no general identification theorem.\n\nFinite outer enclosures require T jointly iid complete cells, N>=2, Dhat, epsilon, conditional-population iid member sampling and whole-cell uncertainty. Shared-member pairs are not independent observations; do not inflate T by the pair count. External p,c coverage boxes, marginal failure budgets and provenance are declared premises. Synthetic fixtures remain synthetic. A denominator approaching zero gives no finite upper bound absent an explicit known deterministic cap; null represents no certified finite upper bound. Calibration budgets must be retained in the union-bound declaration. This parser checks domains and retained conditions, not the numerical coverage inequality or the interval computation. An empty/incompatible confidence region does not unconditionally reject M0.\n\nWeight participation names the published definition K_eff=1/sum(w_i^2) on normalized weights, with range1..active support. It is concentration, not a physical count of independent sources. The value is not recalculated by this profile. Declaration refinement for this object delegates interval inclusion to the existing Obligations continuous lattice, only at identical model, observation, units and numerical context. Other results compare only by exact canonical equality; no new estimator order or meet is invented.\n\nunmeasured with a named reason and no number is valid. A numerical record missing a premise refuses; no automatic zero, confidence or independence is supplied. Canonical JSON keys and record fields use the existing UTF-8 order; golden vectors record exact bytes. Contract and vectors preserve the three original open cards and the empirical unmeasured state. Native vectors capture bounded observations; downstream consumers must verify their own inputs and contracts.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/measure-m0 shape=docs/contracts/measure-m0.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Module inventory\n\nProfile: topos-inventory/1. This is a separate data profile, not a change to bound-lock/1. It reuses the existing lexical canonicalizer and UTF-8 byte order, without Unicode normalization.\n\nEach reached-unit record declares source, version, holder, unit-kind=module, unit and reached-from. The current concrete reach producer selects an explicit exports path from exact package.json bytes: the manifest digest and canonical encoded selector identify reached-from; source/version must match the manifest and the selected value must equal the module path. Unselected/unreached modules emit no line. This proves the selected export edge, not execution, actual use, an import graph or symbol-level reachability.\n\nWith handed module bytes, SHA-256 computes a point digest. Without module bytes, a named open record retains the unit and holder with needs=digest; absence is not an empty file. Merely encoding or decoding a row is syntax inspection, not provenance or availability certification. The digest callback must implement the declared SHA-256.\n\nCanonical bytes order fields and lines; only identical lines deduplicate. Distinct holders, distinct reached-from edges and conflicting handed digests remain distinct records. The sample has 18 public export paths, 15 selected runtime edges and 14 distinct module files.\n\nDiff is exact line-set comparison with an explicit maximum and omitted count. It neither admits nor withdraws acts. There is no transport, signature creation, quorum, permission decision or history fold. Bound retains the fold; public API index integration remains a joint0.2.3 task.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/module-inventory shape=docs/contracts/module-inventory.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Physical lines on declared regions\n\n`topos-physical-lines/1` consumes an explicit named-file snapshot, each file's bytes and digest, and region selectors. The host supplies bytes and a SHA-256 primitive. This pure function reads no files, clock, network or authority store. Its normative contract is `physical-lines.json`.\n\n`readPhysicalLines(input, hash)` reuses the wire selector matcher and Obligations 0.4.7 `finiteRegionMeasure`. It does not reproduce the rational valuation, overlap or union algebra. An atom is a named file in the supplied snapshot, with its physical-line count. No set of region names is built in.\n\nThe text domain is valid UTF-8, with LF separators and CRLF accepted as one separator. Isolated CR refuses. An empty file is zero lines; otherwise LF bytes are counted and an unfinished final line adds one. Blank lines and comments count. A UTF-8 BOM is content; other Unicode separators are not LF.\n\nEvery region retains its scope, selectors, per-selector selection status and concrete member names. A matched union does not hide another unmatched selector: the consumer still sees its named `unmeasured` selection and must not use the union count to pay that selector’s demand. A selector with no members gives `unmeasured` and `selector-no-match`, with no numerical value. An existing empty file can give measured zero. Numeric valuation excludes unmatched regions; its aggregate does not discharge their obligations. Overlaps and unassigned files remain visible. A full-snapshot authority/completeness claim must come from the consumer; an input digest alone does not supply it.\n\nPolicies declare a byte budget and work-row budget. Preparation is outside the measured reader. Byte count is checked before digesting or decoding; selection work is declared separately from the Obligations input-row work. Exhaustion returns a named unmeasured result. These deterministic input units are not runtime promises or permission to widen a consumer ceiling.\n\nThe positive samples use JSON `text` as UTF-8 transport; a native adapter converts it to `Uint8Array`, verifies the declared byte digests and calls the same API. Negative samples alter text without changing its digest. Two unrelated sample namespaces exercise the same contract. Unit vectors additionally cover disjoint and nested supports, malformed selectors, duplicate files, invalid UTF-8, CR handling, final lines and budgets.\n\nBound owns ceiling comparison, witness acceptance, widening and `BLOCK`. Source tests are implementation evidence; the native quartet remains a separate region until its declared reader and binding produce receipts.\n" at=witness:contextual-reading-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/physical-lines shape=docs/contracts/physical-lines.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Reference profile: explicit bootstrap and inspected standing\n\nThis candidate profile encodes one consumed object class, standing, with algorithm sha256, semantic digest, separate transport blob digest and an explicit source URL. Every field occurs once in fixed order; exact encode/decode round trip is required. Unknown classes and algorithms, duplicates, missing or extra fields, credentials, fragments and noncanonical encoding refuse by name. The source is a locator, never authority or independence. Schemes are a host policy; this sample allows https only.\n\nThe transport wrapper is exactly the UTF-8 bytes of JSON.stringify({profile:\"semantic-standing@1\",standing:<canonical text>}), in that property order, with no whitespace or final newline. JSON is decoded by the platform JSON decoder, then exact reconstruction is compared. Duplicate members, including duplicate members whose final parsed value would be valid, refuse even when their blob hash matches. Invalid UTF-8 refuses. This wrapper is a candidate transport profile, not a published universal JSON canonicalization contract.\n\nBlob integrity is verified before wrapper interpretation. Existing Topos readSemanticStanding and semanticStandingBytes validate and project in effective order, then the semantic digest is checked. Bad blob bytes are an integrity refusal, never a fork. Different admitted claims may conflict; this inspector neither admits nor authenticates claims and performs no encounter algebra.\n\nNative samples hand the source response bytes through the reader's declared inputs. The synchronous inspector receives only those handed bytes. The host's node:crypto hash primitive verifies their digests; Topos supplies semantic parsing/projection. No network request occurs in the reader. This pays native inspection of explicitly supplied source bytes, not native content-request transport resolution. The earlier Node/browser bootstrap exercises the HTTP host separately. A native fetching port remains an explicit unpaid integration demand.\n\nThe native direct reader is declared with bounded lifetime and response bytes. Its resource limits are fixture policy. Selection, help, check, preview and sign do not invoke a transport adapter. No key, private store or remote source is required by a clean sample consumer. The package includes the selected reader as public sample support; no private runner is a public use command.\n\nTwo unrelated places use different canonical standings (catalog counts and route notes). Both print attributed conformance readings through Bound's receipts view; these are inspected readings, not states or authenticated authority. Each has a yes place and a no place whose duplicate-member wrapper has a correctly recomputed blob hash. The no place refuses `REFUSE·reference noncanonical document wrapper`. Reader dependencies are bundled from the existing implementation with an explicit module inventory; no algebra is copied or reimplemented.\n\n\n`semantic-standing@1` remains an inspection projection. Its bytes and reference do not authenticate their declarations. An authenticated opening additionally consumes an `authenticated-standing@1` carrier: the UTF-8 bytes of `JSON.stringify([\"authenticated-standing@1\", originals, proof]) + \"\\n\"`, where originals are canonical signed wire lines in supplied order and proof is a canonical signed wire declaration. The carrier is a transport envelope, excluded from standing identity.\n\nThe host selects the authority snapshot, authority epoch, proof scope, trusted public signers and coverage, signature eras, origin and witness policy, and selected form standing and implementation. They are external trust inputs; neither source URL, carrier, digest nor embedded key creates authority. The proof has `role=writes form=alphabet measure=digest`, its value commits to `JSON.stringify([\"authenticated-standing@1\", originals]) + \"\\n\"`, and `at=witness:<snapshot>` binds the selected snapshot. Its signed epoch equals the selected authority epoch. Existing native authority and signature validation applies. Original lines are authenticated historical evidence; the current snapshot proof is evaluated at the host's current authority epoch. This inspection does not authorize reusing an answer as current.\n\nAfter authentication, existing object-history validation applies with both signature admission and the host's declared admission policy. Existing semantic-live@2 cell cones determine live typed membership only. The original globally effective order (ascending epoch, supplied stable order within an epoch) determines standing order. All live configuration remains included. A configuration withdrawal requires the admitted standing fold and otherwise refuses by name; no configuration-history semantics are inferred. Typed authorized withdrawals use the existing validator and live projection. The semantic digest excludes retired typed claims and withdrawal envelopes; it retains configuration and effective order.\n\nThe host fixes finite byte and record budgets and bounds transport before handing over bytes. Shared transport buffers refuse; owned copied bytes are hashed and decoded. Verification completes before prepared records are returned. No writer or commit port is exposed by this opening. Authority-context identity commits to host snapshot, epoch, proof scope, origin/admission policy identity, form implementation identity, signers with coverage and signature algorithms. Form standing and executable implementation remain distinct identities.\n" at=witness:authenticated-reference-native-quartet by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/reference-profile shape=docs/contracts/reference-profile.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Reference with optional location\n\nProfile: #topos-reference:1. This is separate from #bound:1, whose required source and historical bytes remain unchanged.\n\nIdentity fields are class=standing, algorithm=sha256, digest (semantic standing) and blob (exact canonical document bytes). Optional location and location-class must appear together. location-class is viewer or holder. Addresses are canonical absolute HTTP(S) URLs without credentials; holder addresses have no fragment; viewer fragments are preserved. Field order is class, algorithm, digest, blob, location, location-class; values use encodeURIComponent. Duplicate, unknown or malformed fields and unsupported profile versions refuse by name.\n\nThe content-identity projection excludes both location fields. Without handed bytes, the answer is needs/bytes, including an unavailable address or a viewer that is not a raw-byte endpoint. There is no network invocation or availability assertion. With bytes, the declared byte budget, blob integrity, canonical semantic-standing@1 wrapper and standing identity are checked. An integrity mismatch refuses; a syntactically valid locator does not repair it. SHA-256 is supplied through an explicit digest callback.\n\nInspection does not authenticate a signer, publisher, holder or origin, grant permission, certify independence or select a transport. Those operations remain outside this profile.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/reference-location shape=docs/contracts/reference-location.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Region reading of a walk\n\nProfile: topos-region-reading/1. This structural form reuses Topos0.2.2 walkInventory, walkAt, walkHeaders, walkProjection, lexical canonicalization, scope matching and count interpretation. It changes no existing wire or protocol. The concrete producer is the existing pure inventory walk on a synthetic three-region fixture; no consumer application is implemented.\n\nThe exact handed UTF-8 walk-header document is bound by SHA-256. Its lines are canonical, unique, byte-sorted and LF-terminated. Exactly one root and unambiguous scope/measure coordinates are required; each region row refers to that root. The frame is declared canonical-walk-headers@1 with an explicit inventory resolution profile. No projection is inferred from a numeric resolution label.\n\nSelectors use the existing scope matcher, plus the explicit measure filter all/count/digest. Every selector must match at least one line after that filter; an empty selection refuses and never becomes zero. Overlapping selections union exact lines once. The result names every matched line and its SHA-256 of line+LF, and binds the complete selection digest. SHA-256 callback correctness is a caller premise.\n\nUnit canonical-lines counts selected canonical records. Unit declared-walk-count sums exact nonnegative count intervals and requires an explicit count unit; mixed digest/count selections refuse. It reports counts stated in the handed inventory, not independently verified physical contents. A nonempty selected count record with value0..0 legitimately yields zero. Ambiguous coordinates, unknown units, malformed frames and missing coverage refuse.\n\nAn evidence declaration includes keyClass=attest, producer, provenance and finite selected-scope coverage. Each selected scope must fall inside that declared coverage. The class label does not authenticate the producer, verify a CI run, authorize transport or establish unrestricted coverage. Authentication and execution remain not established; statistical confidence is not applicable. A wider selector reading is not a grant of authority.\n\nGolden vectors independently identify matched lines/counts and exact output bytes for the actual existing producer output. Two places are used for candidate checks. Native reader captures, admission, integrated public exports and real downstream consumer/CI verification remain pending. The three original statistical cards and empirical unmeasured state remain open.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/region-reading shape=docs/contracts/region-reading.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Registry as a governed place\n\nProfile: topos-registry-place/1. A pure declaration of lineage admission or exact withdrawal within a named place, reusing canonical text, attributed content identity and the existing source-card interpreter. It changes no wire or protocol, and does not compute live standing.\n\nEach inscription names place, holder and lineage. An admission can declare shares, a source-card content digest, a horizon and a quorum requirement. Two holders naming the same lineage are two distinct inscriptions, with distinct identities. Only the same exact canonical line deduplicates. Lapxo is an ordinary holder; there is no privileged name.\n\nAn explicit shares list, including an empty list, remains a holder declaration, never proof of causal independence or M0 weights. Missing shares stays open. A handed source card is checked by content digest and interpreted under the selected source-card catalog. Its authority is still declared, not authenticated. A holder's shares declaration does not rewrite the card publisher's own declaration.\n\nHorizon specifies an inclusive nonnegative integer interval under a named clock and unit. Quorum vocabulary is named-threshold@1 with unique named eligible holders and a positive required threshold no larger than that list. Missing horizon, quorum, card or shares produces explicit open demands; no defaults are invented. Invalid supplied requirements refuse. The form neither reads a clock nor decides expiry or counts approvals. Requirements declared on an entry do not replace an admitted place policy.\n\nA withdrawal names the SHA-256 of one exact admission's canonical UTF-8 line plus LF. When target bytes are handed, the content digest and admission kind are checked; place, holder and lineage must match. A withdrawal cannot structurally target another holder's attribution. Missing target bytes stays open. This identity check proves no actor is authorized; the protocol must authenticate and decide permission under the real governance. There is no fallback to a prefix, coordinate, latest value or another holder.\n\nregistryLines is only a byte-sorted set of exact inscriptions. Admission and withdrawal both remain present; no removal, winner selection, quorum enforcement, expiration, signature, transport, charging or ledger fold occurs here. Signing and execution belong to the protocol. Signature/approval fields are not accepted as if they were requirements.\n\nVectors cover two holders, exact-line idempotence, retained withdrawal history, explicit no-sharing without inferred independence, missing inputs as named open demands, invalid governance, changed attribution and corrupt content. The concrete local producer is the pure canonical inscription encoder with handed public card/target bytes; the independent fixture reader uses the interpreted identities and collections. External protocol/consumer integration, native captures, admission and joint public exports remain pending. The three original statistical cards and empirical unmeasured state remain unchanged.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/registry-place shape=docs/contracts/registry-place.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Source card\n\nProfile: topos-source-card/1. This standalone shape uses the existing lexical canonicalizer and selected Topos forms; it changes neither bound-lock/1 nor #bound:1.\n\nA card declares source, publisher, catalog identity, form, canonical JSON parameters, resolution, authority reference, cost, cost-unit, cost-limit, needs and optional shares. API, sensor, CSV, model and person use the same interpretation. Authority is a declaration, not authentication or a permission grant. The canonical UTF-8 line is the signable content; key selection, signing, verification and transport remain protocol operations.\n\nThe selected provider supplies the catalog identity and actual form decoders. A digest alone cannot authenticate their binding. This fixture pins published Topos0.2.2 interval and budget modules. Unknown forms and missing authority refuse by name. Resolution is parsed through that selected form and must be canonical and nonempty. Cost reuses budgetForm over [0,cost-limit], with explicit unit; it is a resource bound, not a statistical measure.\n\nWithin identical attribution, catalog, form parameters, units, needs, shares and opaque extensions, refinement is the product order of the selected resolution lattice and the existing budget lattice. Different attribution is incomparable; there is no cross-source aggregation or authority precedence. Order is supplied by Obligations0.4.6 via these existing forms, not reimplemented here.\n\nAbsent shares yields unknown-shared-cause and independence not established. Present shares is only a declaration, including the word none; neither case proves independence or supplies M0 weights. Needs uses none for an explicitly empty list. Declared lists deduplicate and sort; opaque string fields are preserved and affect content identity and refinement compatibility. Protocol envelope fields are refused. There is no Unicode normalization.\n\nVectors specify exact UTF-8 bytes, five source kinds, narrower and incompatible refinements, two public sample places and named negative cases. Native captures, selected reader binding, admission and central public exports remain pending. No algebraic law, successful external consumer or public availability is asserted from these fixtures.\n" at=witness:public-profile-delivery by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/source-card shape=docs/contracts/source-card.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# bound-lock/1-compatibility\n\nReuse existing lexical and epoch-sensitive wire expectations; no wire or algebra change.\n\n- Golden lexical cases, UTF-8 distinction and effective-epoch field cases use the existing parser, canonicalizer and wireAt/fromLine implementation.\n- No new field, grammar or old-profile extension is introduced by the new contract profiles.\n- An altered expected canonical byte string refuses rather than accepting a new baseline.\n- Retirement/publication mechanics are retained evidence, not a newly invented inverse or syntax.\n\n## External obligations\n\n- A producer not represented by these fixtures must provide its own exact compatibility vector\n" at=witness:reference-wire-catalog-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/wire-compatibility shape=docs/contracts/wire-compatibility.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# topos-portable-birth/1\n\nDescribe a bounded public birth payload without transferring admission or local authority.\n\n- Origin digest plus canonical unsigned alphabet writes; byte and item budgets supplied by caller.\n- Only declared public-line fields are permitted; by/sig/epoch envelopes and authority/key/root/store/derived material are refused.\n- No filesystem, store, signer, root, historical admission or automatic installation is part of the profile.\n- Occupied coordinates produce named open pack/collision needs, never replacement.\n- Arbitrary data cannot be certified free of secrets by syntax: the public-data producer remains responsible for selection; the receiver never executes or lands these lines by inspection.\n- All original declaration bytes are preserved; incompatible declarations remain for the native consumer to judge.\n\n## Consumer obligations\n\n- Origin authentication\n- Consumer collision resolution and native coverage/admission\n- Public-data selection; no generic secret-detection claim\n\nThe native vector captures positive and negative declarations at catalog/items and orchard/harvest. Parsing or calculation establishes no authority, admission, stored effect or release approval.\n" at=witness:portable-source-profile-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/portable-birth shape=docs/contracts/portable-birth.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# topos-profile-composition/1\n\nDescribe finite parent-by-digest profiles, declared field contracts and unambiguous category pins.\n\n- Parent bytes are supplied by caller, checked by digest, decoded canonically and traversed under an explicit item/closure budget; no I/O or universal region list.\n- Missing parents remain needs/bytes; corrupted bytes refuse integrity; cycles refuse.\n- Each field names its semantic contract digest; receiver selects the PolicyField with matching coordinate. Uncatalogued semantics refuse.\n- Existing meet/order classifies narrowing or widening. Widening and non-meet replacement leave authorize-transition needs; adding a field is addition, never called narrowing.\n- A witness remains a reading declaration requiring verify-witness; attest never grants authority. A witness does not erase authorize-transition needs.\n- Category pins name category, class, algorithm and digest. Ambiguous inherited/own pins refuse instead of last-wins.\n- All ancestor constraints remain visible; inherited incompatible non-meet declarations are not silently overridden. No inverse of a reduced aggregate is reconstructed.\n\n## Consumer obligations\n\n- Witness signature/CI provenance and consuming policy acceptance\n- Authorization and native admission of widening or added permissions\n- Resolving incompatible inherited profiles or replacing category pins\n\nThe native vector captures positive and negative declarations at catalog/items and orchard/harvest. Parsing or calculation establishes no authority, admission, stored effect or release approval.\n" at=witness:portable-source-profile-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/profile-composition shape=docs/contracts/profile-composition.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# topos-reference-projection/1\n\nA separate bounded inline preview and declared relation profile; historical references retain their exact meaning.\n\n- #bound:1 and #topos-reference:1 bytes/constructors remain unchanged. The new wrapper never inserts syntax into either historical profile.\n- Receiver declares inline UTF-8 byte limit; overflow refuses, never truncates. Inline text has an exact digest.\n- Duplicate relations require explicit preserve/refuse. Relation names and target digests are declarations, not truth.\n- Preview bytes never replace complete standing bytes or authenticate their origin. Full content remains needs/bytes/<blob>.\n- A fragment is readable by page code; secrecy is not promised. Producers must not include secrets inline.\n- Holder and viewer remain distinct locator classes; a viewer URL is not assumed to be a raw-byte endpoint. Availability, byte integrity and admitted disagreement remain different.\n- The existing contextual meaning of none is retained; there is no silent new literal rule.\n\n## External obligations\n\n- Projection interpretation/completeness, relation verification and authenticity\n- Transport/origin policy and actual content resolution\n" at=witness:reference-wire-catalog-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/reference-compatibility shape=docs/contracts/reference-compatibility.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# topos-refusal-catalog/1\n\nMachine-readable exact producer/code catalog for the declared new-form surface, with observed producer vectors and separately open external demands.\n\n- vectors/refusal-catalog.json is the data source. This Markdown table is derived; no consumer parses prose.\n- Every local entry names producer, implementation digest, exact code, class, meaning, next, vector and case. All65 local causes were observed in two unrelated contexts.\n- Next names resolve to catalog actions; duplicate producer/code pairs, unresolved next and malformed definitions refuse. No executable action or authority is carried by next.\n- Catalog identity preimage is encodeRefusalCatalog(data,budget) followed by one LF. Authority/action context commits this identity alongside standingContract; the receiver selects both.\n- Lookup returns declared metadata, never producer authenticity or authority. Unknown/external producer codes remain named open needs.\n- This scope covers the declared0.2.4 new forms. Legacy wire/reference cause bytes remain in compatibility fixtures, without inventing slash aliases.\n- Five Head door codes, github-runs-on and doc-install-region remain seven named producer demands until their actual contract/version/vector arrives.\n- The older governance-refusals draft is historical evidence; it is not the newly selected catalog.\n\n| Producer | Code | Meaning | Next |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Topos | `action/bytes` | Native evidence exceeds its caller byte budget. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `action/consumer-context` | The receiver did not supply the native act-verification ports. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `action/context` | Action statement differs from the receiver-expected statement. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `action/link-binding` | Emitter, region or reference identity differs from the committed statement. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `action/receipt-binding` | Act identity or receipt digest differs from verified native evidence. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `action/records` | Native evidence contains a non-string record. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `action/statement-binding` | The verified act does not contain the required signed statement commitment. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `authority-card/clock` | Issue time is outside the declared horizon or is invalid. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `authority-card/consumer-context` | Receiver time or judgment-verification context is absent. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `authority-card/decision` | Decision is not allow, ask or deny. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `authority-card/duplicate-action` | One exact request occurs more than once. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `card-stale` | The selected context or explicit clock does not match the card. | `refresh-card` |\n| Topos | `catalog/duplicate-action` | A next-action name occurs twice. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `catalog/duplicate-code` | A producer/code pair is duplicated. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `catalog/duplicate-demand` | An external producer/demand pair is duplicated. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `catalog/prose` | A meaning is empty or contains control characters. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `catalog/unresolved-next` | An entry names a next action absent from the catalog. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `governance/change-kind` | The predecessor, successor and decision record kinds do not match. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `governance/duplicate-proof-key` | Two proof references name the same signer key. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `governance/kind` | The record is not a declared group, member or decision. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `governance/prior-group-binding` | Successor and proposal do not bind the prior group under the same place/name. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `not-in-card` | The exact request digest is absent from the card. | `request-card-entry` |\n| Topos | `origin/consumer-context` | The receiver did not supply a coverage-verification port. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `origin/context` | Attributed key or context differs from the receiver-selected context. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `pack/authority-material` | Birth payload attempts to include authority material or a withdrawal. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `pack/canonical-declaration` | Birth declaration bytes are not their canonical wire encoding. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `pack/declaration` | A birth declaration is not a single valid wire line. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `pack/duplicate` | An identical birth declaration is repeated. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `pack/empty` | A birth payload must contain at least one declaration. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `pack/envelope` | The birth declaration has an unapproved field, role, form or authority envelope. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/ambiguous-pin` | Inherited and local pins conflict within one category. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/closure-budget` | Finite parent traversal exceeds its declared closure-item budget. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/cycle` | Parent traversal contains a cycle, including with an untrusted digest provider. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/duplicate-coordinate` | A field, category or witness coordinate is repeated. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/duplicate-parent` | A parent digest is repeated. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/field-contract` | Inherited declarations of a field name different semantic contracts. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/integrity` | Supplied parent bytes do not match the named digest. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/pin-algorithm` | A category pin uses an unsupported digest algorithm. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/undeclared-field` | Receiver has no selected semantic field contract for this coordinate. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `profile/value` | A field value is not a string. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/budget` | Caller byte/item budgets are missing or invalid. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/bytes` | Encoded bytes exceed the caller byte budget. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/canonical` | Supplied bytes differ from the selected canonical bytes. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/coordinate` | A logical coordinate contains an absolute path, traversal or empty segment. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/digest` | Expected a lowercase SHA-256 identifier with 64 hexadecimal digits. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/duplicate` | A named collection member is repeated. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/fields` | A required field is missing or an undeclared field was provided. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/items` | Expected an array within the declared item budget. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/json` | A structured field is not valid JSON. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/line` | The underlying wire record cannot be parsed. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/name` | Expected a nonempty name without whitespace or control characters. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/object` | Expected an object record rather than null, array or scalar. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `record/profile` | The record prefix differs from the selected profile. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `reference-projection/budget` | The receiver inline byte limit is not a nonnegative safe integer. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `reference-projection/duplicate-relation` | An exact relation repeats under the refuse policy. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `reference-projection/duplicates-policy` | Duplicate handling is neither preserve nor refuse. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `reference-projection/inline-budget` | UTF-8 projection bytes exceed the receiver limit; nothing is truncated. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `reference-projection/inline-integrity` | Inline projection bytes do not match their declared digest. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `reference-projection/text` | Inline projection is not text. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `source-exhaustion/context` | Card identity, source, cost unit or receiver clock/unit differs. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `source-exhaustion/duplicate-event` | One event ID is repeated in the exhaustion declaration. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `source-exhaustion/frame` | Exhaustion or known-since time is outside the declared frame. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `source-exhaustion/recovery` | Recovery is neither unknown nor declared. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `source-exhaustion/recovery-before-exhaustion` | Declared recovery precedes exhaustion. | `correct-declared-input` |\n| Topos | `source-exhaustion/time` | An exhaustion timestamp is not a nonnegative safe integer. | `correct-declared-input` |\n\n## Open producer demands\n\n- Head/door-bind: Producer version, exact meaning, next and executable vector; no compatible-producer claim.\n- Head/door-host: Producer version, exact meaning, next and executable vector; no compatible-producer claim.\n- Head/door-origin: Producer version, exact meaning, next and executable vector; no compatible-producer claim.\n- Head/door-unasked: Producer version, exact meaning, next and executable vector; no compatible-producer claim.\n- Head/door-founded: Producer version, exact meaning, next and executable vector; no compatible-producer claim.\n- topos-github/github-runs-on: Producer contract and vector for runner selection.\n- unresolved-producer/doc-install-region: Identify the producer and supply its installation-region contract and vector.\n" at=witness:reference-wire-catalog-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/refusal-catalog shape=docs/contracts/refusal-catalog.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# topos-source-exhaustion/1\n\nRepresent source exhaustion, attributed events and known/unknown recovery without implementing budgets or scheduling.\n\n- Binds the exact source card, source, cost unit, observation evidence, explicit exhaustedAt and knownSince in a declared frame.\n- Events have distinct IDs and evidence digests; duplication refuses rather than charging twice.\n- Declared recovery names its time and evidence; unknown recovery has neither invented time nor evidence.\n- Recovery cannot precede exhaustion; consumer-selected source, clock and time unit must match.\n- Cost unit and time unit are distinct. No Date.now, network, mutable counters or retry scheduler.\n- Source-card decoding names the declaration; source-card form conformance, observation authentication, actual budget exhaustion and acceptance remain consumer obligations.\n\n## Consumer obligations\n\n- Signature or provenance of events and exhaustion\n- Source-card catalog/form conformance\n- Ceiling comparison, eligibility to retry and credit resets\n\nThe native vector captures positive and negative declarations at catalog/items and orchard/harvest. Parsing or calculation establishes no authority, admission, stored effect or release approval.\n" at=witness:portable-source-profile-contracts by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/contracts/source-exhaustion shape=docs/contracts/source-exhaustion.md value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 176\" width=\"640\" height=\"176\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A configuration record, shown field by field\">\n<style>\ntext{font:13px ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace;fill:#59636e}.field{fill:#2da44e;font-weight:600}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){text{fill:#9198a1}}\n</style>\n<text class=\"field\" x=\"104\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"end\">scope</text>\n<text x=\"116\" y=\"30\">wire/line/fields</text>\n<text class=\"field\" x=\"104\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"end\">role</text>\n<text x=\"116\" y=\"56\">reads</text>\n<text class=\"field\" x=\"104\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"end\">measure</text>\n<text x=\"116\" y=\"82\">count</text>\n<text class=\"field\" x=\"104\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"end\">form</text>\n<text x=\"116\" y=\"108\">interval</text>\n<text class=\"field\" x=\"104\" y=\"134\" text-anchor=\"end\">value</text>\n<text x=\"116\" y=\"134\">7..16</text>\n<text class=\"field\" x=\"104\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"end\">at</text>\n<text x=\"116\" y=\"160\">policy:wire/fields</text>\n</svg>\n" at=policy:figures by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=figure/line/svg value=declared
bound-lock/1 about="<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 184\" width=\"640\" height=\"184\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"21.2..21.4 · free and radiator apart · conflict\">\n<style>\ntext{font:14px ui-sans-serif,-apple-system,\"Segoe UI\",Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;fill:#59636e}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){text{fill:#9198a1}}\n</style>\n<line x1=\"143.7\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"496.3\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#8c959f\"/>\n<rect x=\"143.7\" y=\"8\" width=\"62.2\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8c959f\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"164.4\" y=\"36\" width=\"20.7\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8c959f\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"164.4\" y=\"64\" width=\"20.7\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8c959f\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"454.8\" y=\"108\" width=\"41.5\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8c959f\"></rect>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"144\">21.2..21.4 · free</text>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"168\">radiator apart · conflict</text>\n</svg>\n" at=policy:figures by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=figure/fold/svg value=declared
bound-lock/1 about="<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 720 190\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A missing declared artifact is refused without substituting local bytes\"><style>text{font:15px ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif;fill:#526078}rect{fill:#f6f8fc;stroke:#ccd6e6}path{fill:none;stroke:#9dabc0;stroke-width:1.8}@media(prefers-color-scheme:dark){text{fill:#b7c6dd}rect{fill:#111d30;stroke:#334660}}</style><rect x=\"18\" y=\"46\" width=\"206\" height=\"94\" rx=\"18\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"84\">Declared standing</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"111\">names artifact bytes</text><path d=\"M224 94H284\"/><rect x=\"284\" y=\"46\" width=\"175\" height=\"94\" rx=\"18\"/><text x=\"305\" y=\"84\">Artifact absent</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"111\">identity unresolved</text><path d=\"M459 94H512\"/><rect x=\"512\" y=\"46\" width=\"190\" height=\"94\" rx=\"18\"/><text x=\"532\" y=\"84\">REFUSE</text><text x=\"532\" y=\"111\">no local substitute</text></svg>" at=policy:figures by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=figure/refuses/svg value=declared
bound-lock/1 about="<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 340\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"title desc\">\n<title id=\"title\">One standing, many interpretations</title><desc id=\"desc\">Wire, forms, classes and views are parts of a selected topos. Its standing identity is independent of host transport. Artifact dependencies are verified by digest.</desc>\n<style>text{font:15px ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif;fill:#526078}.label{font-size:18px;font-weight:600;fill:#23304c}.caption{font-size:13px}rect{fill:#f6f8fc;stroke:#ccd6e6}path{fill:none;stroke:#8b9cbc;stroke-width:1.6}.pin{fill:#e8e5fa;stroke:#a69cdb}@media(prefers-color-scheme:dark){text{fill:#aab9d1}.label{fill:#e0e8f7}rect{fill:#111d30;stroke:#334660}.pin{fill:#272347;stroke:#7469a7}}</style>\n<g><rect x=\"25\" y=\"22\" width=\"155\" height=\"63\" rx=\"16\"/><text class=\"label\" x=\"46\" y=\"50\">Wire</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"71\">record grammar</text>\n<rect x=\"25\" y=\"99\" width=\"155\" height=\"63\" rx=\"16\"/><text class=\"label\" x=\"46\" y=\"127\">Forms</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"148\">value interpretation</text>\n<rect x=\"25\" y=\"176\" width=\"155\" height=\"63\" rx=\"16\"/><text class=\"label\" x=\"46\" y=\"204\">Classes</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"225\">allowed references</text>\n<rect x=\"25\" y=\"253\" width=\"155\" height=\"63\" rx=\"16\"/><text class=\"label\" x=\"46\" y=\"281\">Views</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"302\">result presentation</text></g>\n<path d=\"M180 54 C250 54 240 170 300 170 M180 131 C250 131 240 170 300 170 M180 208 C250 208 240 170 300 170 M180 285 C250 285 240 170 300 170\"/>\n<rect class=\"pin\" x=\"300\" y=\"121\" width=\"190\" height=\"98\" rx=\"24\"/><text class=\"label\" x=\"326\" y=\"156\">Folded standing</text><text x=\"326\" y=\"181\">canonical live claims</text><text x=\"326\" y=\"203\">selected by digest</text>\n<path d=\"M490 170 C525 170 524 95 565 95 M490 170 C525 170 524 255 565 255\"/>\n<rect x=\"565\" y=\"54\" width=\"170\" height=\"80\" rx=\"18\"/><text class=\"label\" x=\"584\" y=\"87\">Interpretation</text><text x=\"584\" y=\"111\">no runtime required</text>\n<rect x=\"565\" y=\"214\" width=\"170\" height=\"80\" rx=\"18\"/><text class=\"label\" x=\"584\" y=\"248\">Host</text><text x=\"584\" y=\"272\">resolve artifact bytes</text>\n<text class=\"caption\" x=\"301\" y=\"260\">A capsule is an offer.</text><text class=\"caption\" x=\"301\" y=\"280\">It is not the identity of the topos.</text>\n</svg>" at=policy:figures by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=figure/world/svg value=declared
